From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2object: offer the colorimetry-free caps variant for encoded formats too

add_non_colorimetry_caps() exists so that negotiation still succeeds when the
colorimetry detected upstream is not one the device advertises. It only applies
that fallback to video/x-raw structures, but a decoder's OUTPUT (encoded) queue
also answers TRY_FMT with a colorspace, so the probed colorimetry list is placed
on the encoded sink caps as well. An upstream element that knows only part of
the colorimetry can then never intersect with them.

matroskademux hits this: for VP8/VP9 it emits

  video/x-vp9, width=1280, height=720, framerate=30/1,
               interlace-mode=progressive, colorimetry=(string)2:0:0:0

while the probed sink caps of v4l2vp9dec are an enumerated list of fully
specified values:

  video/x-vp9, width=[144,8192,2], height=[144,8192,2],
    colorimetry=(string){ bt601, 1:4:16:3, smpte240m, bt709, 1:3:5:1,
                          2:4:5:2, 2:4:5:3, 1:4:5:3, 2:4:7:1, bt2020,
                          bt2100-pq }

The intersection is empty and the pads never link, so the pipeline fails with
"not-linked". H.264/H.265/AV1 are unaffected only because h264parse/h265parse/
av1parse normalise colorimetry before it reaches the decoder; VP8 and VP9 have
no parser element to do so.

Reproduced on real content, on an Arm CIX Sky1 (CD8180) stateful V4L2 decoder
running GStreamer 1.28.5, which advertises VP80 and VP90 on its OUTPUT queue.

The cleanest demonstration is a stream copy, so the video bitstream is byte
identical and only the muxer differs. Big Buck Bunny as published by
test-videos.co.uk was muxed by Lavf58.14.100 and carries no colorimetry at all;
remuxing it with ffmpeg 8.1.2 (Lavf62) adds a partially specified Colour
element and the same bitstream stops negotiating:

  ffmpeg -i Big_Buck_Bunny_1080_10s_5MB.webm -c copy bbb-remux.webm

  original  -> (no colorimetry field)      v4l2vp9dec: Got EOS
  remuxed   -> colorimetry=(string)2:0:0:0 v4l2vp9dec: not-linked

This is not a synthetic-file problem: current ffmpeg writes that partial Colour
element, so ordinary WebM produced today triggers it while older files do not.

  # before - fails
  gst-launch-1.0 filesrc location=vp9-720p.webm ! matroskademux ! \
      v4l2vp9dec ! fakesink
  ERROR: streaming stopped, reason not-linked (-1)

  # confirms colorimetry is the only obstacle
  gst-launch-1.0 filesrc location=vp9-720p.webm ! matroskademux ! \
      capssetter caps="video/x-vp9,colorimetry=bt709" ! v4l2vp9dec ! fakesink
  Got EOS

  # after this patch, unmodified pipeline
  gst-launch-1.0 filesrc location=vp9-720p.webm ! matroskademux ! \
      v4l2vp9dec ! fakesink
  Got EOS

VP8 behaves identically (not-linked before, EOS after). Hardware decoding is
used in both cases; without the patch the only working path is the software
vp9dec/vp8dec fallback, which makes the failure easy to miss because playbin
silently degrades to software rather than erroring.

The most important consequence is that autoplug degrades SILENTLY. v4l2vp9dec is
ranked primary+1 (257), above the software vp9dec (256), so decodebin tries the
hardware decoder first - and when the caps do not intersect it simply falls back
to software and reports success. Measured with `gst-launch-1.0 -v ... !
matroskademux ! decodebin ! fakesink` on the remuxed Big Buck Bunny above:

  stock  + colorimetry present  -> GstVP9Dec:vp9dec0   (software)  Got EOS
  patched+ colorimetry present  -> v4l2vp9dec          (hardware)  Got EOS
  stock  + colorimetry absent   -> v4l2vp9dec          (hardware)  Got EOS

Every one of those returns EOS, so nothing in the logs indicates a problem. On a
board where hardware decode is the entire point, users lose it without any error
at all - which is how this went unnoticed here.

Scope note: in a browser (WebKit/Epiphany) this failure surfaces as
MEDIA_ERR_SRC_NOT_SUPPORTED, and the patch clears that. It does not fix every
VP9 playback problem on this platform - a separate stall at currentTime ~0.15
with readyState 2 persists afterwards, and that one reproduces with the
software decoder and with files carrying no colorimetry, so it is not caused by
this bug.

---
diff --git a/subprojects/gst-plugins-good/sys/v4l2/gstv4l2object.c b/subprojects/gst-plugins-good/sys/v4l2/gstv4l2object.c
index 7c84365..b6702f0 100644
--- a/subprojects/gst-plugins-good/sys/v4l2/gstv4l2object.c
+++ b/subprojects/gst-plugins-good/sys/v4l2/gstv4l2object.c
@@ -1823,8 +1823,14 @@ add_non_colorimetry_caps (GstV4l2Object * v4l2object, GstCaps * caps)
     GstStructure *structure = gst_caps_get_structure (caps, i);
     GstCapsFeatures *features = gst_caps_get_features (caps, i);
 
-    if (gst_structure_has_name (structure, "video/x-raw")
-        && gst_structure_has_field (structure, "colorimetry")) {
+    /* Encoded formats carry a probed colorimetry list too: a decoder's OUTPUT
+       queue still answers TRY_FMT with a colorspace, so the list lands on the
+       encoded sink caps. An upstream element that only knows part of the
+       colorimetry then cannot negotiate at all - matroskademux emits
+       colorimetry=2:0:0:0 for VP8/VP9, which unlike H.264/H.265/AV1 have no
+       parser element to normalise it, and the intersection is empty. Offer the
+       colorimetry-free variant for encoded caps as well, not just video/x-raw. */
+    if (gst_structure_has_field (structure, "colorimetry")) {
       GstStructure *alt_s = gst_structure_copy (structure);
       gst_structure_remove_field (alt_s, "colorimetry");
       if (gst_caps_features_contains (features,
